### 12.4 Reasoning effort and escalation

- **Routine:** use a small/balanced model with low or standard effort for exact extraction, formatting, known-pattern edits and short summaries. Clear instructions and an executable check usually matter more than additional reasoning.
- **Normal development:** use a balanced model at medium effort where supported, or the provider's normal adaptive setting. Supply the relevant code, design constraints, expected behavior and completion checks.
- **Complex or consequential:** use Sol or Opus at high effort, Gemini Pro at Extended thinking, or Kimi K3 at Advanced/high, using the selected interface's controls. Examples include pity state machines, save migrations, purchases, cross-platform crashes and tightly coupled combat systems.
- **Exceptional:** reserve maximum effort for a concrete unresolved problem after evidence and ordinary higher-effort work are insufficient. More thinking cannot supply missing files, tool permissions, owner decisions or unavailable engine APIs.
- **Escalate on evidence:** after one substantial failed attempt, identify missing context, tool failure, unclear requirements or reasoning failure. Correct that cause. After two materially similar failures, change the approach or model rather than repeating the same prompt. Skip directly to a stronger model when the consequences justify it.
- **De-escalate when work becomes mechanical:** retain the accepted plan and let a cheaper worker perform bounded execution. Keep a stronger reviewer for the parts whose failure would be costly.

Effort names are provider-specific. OpenAI's checked Astra API supports low/medium/high/xhigh/max; a host can expose a different subset. Claude's newer models use adaptive thinking and provider-supported effort controls; Haiku does not share every control. Gemini 3.8 Flash supports low/medium/high, not minimal. Kimi app labels and Kimi Code API effort values differ. Always use the selected interface's supported values; never copy a provider's parameter names into another provider's request. Sources: [OpenAI catalog](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models), [Claude overview](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/overview), [Gemini Flash](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models/gemini-3.8-flash), [Kimi Code](https://www.kimi.com/code/docs/en/kimi-code/models.html).

